course: dictionary
lesson: 1106
title: One word, many meanings: brass
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words wear different coats. The word brass can be a metal, a kind of instrument, a word for money, or a word for bold rudeness. Read the words around it to find out which one fits. «Where hands rub a brass door knob or a statue's nose every day, the thin dull layer is rubbed off, and the metal stays bright.» Door knob and metal are the clues, so brass here is the shiny metal.
explain: Music gives different clues. «Orchestras usually seat the quieter strings at the front and the louder brass and percussion at the back.» Strings and percussion are other kinds of instruments, so brass here means the instruments, like trumpets and trombones, that you blow into.
explain: Some meanings are informal, so the feel of the sentence tells you what they mean. «He had the brass neck to ask for more» does not mean he had a metal neck. It means he was rudely bold. Always test your meaning by putting it into the sentence and checking it still makes sense.
fact: Brass is a shiny yellow metal made by mixing copper and zinc.
fact: Brass is an alloy, a mix of metals. It does not rust like iron.
fact: The brass, or brass instruments, are musical instruments like trumpets and trombones that you blow into.
fact: The trumpet is brass because your lips must buzz. The saxophone is made of brass but is a woodwind, because it uses a reed.
fact: In everyday talk, brass can mean money (UK slang). The top brass are the most important people in charge. Brass neck means bold rudeness.
exercise: «Where hands rub a brass door knob every day, the metal stays bright.» What is brass in this sentence? | *A shiny yellow metal | Dull, rusty iron | A musical instrument | A kind of drum
exercise: «Orchestras usually seat the louder brass and percussion at the back.» What does brass mean here? | *Musical instruments you blow into | A shiny door handle | Bold rudeness | Money
exercise: «He had the brass neck to ask for more.» What does brass neck mean? | A kind of music | *Bold rudeness | A metal neck | Money for a sweet
test: Which phrase means the most important people in charge? | Brass tacks | *Top brass | Brass band | Brass neck
test: Dan says «get down to brass tacks» means start a band. What does it really mean? | Polish the door knobs | Count the money | Play loud music | *Talk about the real facts
exam: Which of these is a woodwind, even though it is often made of metal? | *Flute | Trumpet | Trombone | Tuba
